INT. COURTHOUSE CORRIDOR - OUTSIDE ROOM 4B - DAY

A long marble hallway. Benches. A water fountain that nobody uses. A clock ticking too loudly.

A MAN sits alone on the far bench, a folder in his lap, a briefcase at his feet. Hands folded. He looks at nobody.

ATTORNEY GWENDOLEN ADEYEMI-STRAND (40s, charcoal suit, a stack of exhibits under one arm) approaches, stops two benches away, and sits. She does not look at him either.

After a long moment, he speaks.

MAN
Do I know you?

GWENDOLEN
You've seen my brief.

MAN
Yes.

GWENDOLEN
Then you know me.

He nods. A bailiff, OFFICER PRIMROSE OKONJO-HALE (30s, uniform pressed, a ring of keys), passes with a clipboard. She glances at them both, slows, then moves on.

MAN
(quietly)
Is it always this quiet?

GWENDOLEN
Before the verdict? Yes.

MAN
After?

GWENDOLEN
Depends on the verdict.

He shifts the folder on his knees. His thumbs press its edges. The clock ticks. Gwendolen sets her exhibits down and straightens them, edges aligned.

MAN
I'm Everett.

GWENDOLEN
Mr. Rutherford-Lowe. I know.

EVERETT
I didn't think you would.

GWENDOLEN
I read the file.

EVERETT
All of it?

GWENDOLEN
All of it.

EVERETT
Then you know what I did.

GWENDOLEN
I know what you're accused of.

He smiles at the floor. A small, tired smile. The door to 4B opens. Officer Primrose Okonjo-Hale stands there, holding it.

PRIMROSE
Counsel. Mr. Rutherford-Lowe. They're ready.

Everett stands. So does Gwendolen. She collects her exhibits. For a moment they stand shoulder to shoulder, facing the door.

GWENDOLEN
Mr. Rutherford-Lowe.

EVERETT
Yes?

GWENDOLEN
Whatever happens, keep your hands where they are.

He looks down. His hands, folded, are trembling. He tightens them. They go still.
